#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE THIRD STAGE

Now I saw in my dream that the highway up which Christian was to go was fenced in on either side by a wall, and that wall was called Salvation. Isa 26:1. Up this way, then, burdened Christian ran, but not without great difficulty, because of the load on his back.

He ran like this till he came to a place where the ground rose a little; and on that place stood a cross, and a little below, at the bottom, a tomb. So I saw in my dream that just as Christian came up to the cross, his burden came loose from his shoulders and fell off his back, and began to tumble, and kept on tumbling till it came to the mouth of the tomb, where it fell in, and I saw it no more.

Then Christian was glad and light-hearted, and said with a merry heart, “He has given me rest by his sorrow, and life by his death.” Then he stood still a while, to look and wonder; for it was very surprising to him that the sight of the cross should ease him of his burden like this. So he looked, and looked again, until the springs that were in his head sent the waters down his cheeks. Zech 12:10. Now as he stood looking and weeping, behold, three Shining Ones came to him, and greeted him with, “Peace be to you.” Then the first said to him, “Your sins are forgiven,” Mark 2:5; the second stripped him of his rags, and clothed him with a change of clothes, Zech 3:4; the third set a mark on his forehead, Eph 1:13, and gave him a roll with a seal on it, which he told him to look at as he ran, and to hand in at the celestial gate; and so they went their way. Then Christian gave three leaps for joy, and went on singing,

#quote[“Thus far did I come laden with my sin, \
Nor could aught ease the grief that I was in, \
Till I came hither. What a place is this! \
Must here be the beginning of my bliss? \
Must here the burden fall from off my back? \
Must here the strings that bound it to me crack? \
Blest cross! blest sepulchre! blest rather be \
The Man that there was put to shame for me!”]

Then I saw in my dream that he went on like this until he came to the bottom, where he saw, a little off the way, three men fast asleep, with fetters on their heels. One was named Simple, another Sloth, and the third Presumption.

Seeing them lying there like this, Christian went to them to see if he could wake them, and cried, You#super[+] are like those who sleep on the top of a mast, Prov 23:34, for the Dead Sea is under you, a gulf that has no bottom: so wake up and come away; be willing, too, and I will help you off with your irons. He also told them, If he who goes about like a roaring lion, 1 Pet 5:8, comes by, you#super[+] will certainly become a prey to his teeth. At that they looked at him, and began to answer him like this: Simple said, I see no danger; Sloth said, Just a little more sleep; and Presumption said, Every tub must stand on its own bottom. And so they lay down to sleep again, and Christian went on his way.

Yet it troubled him to think that men in such danger should think so little of the kindness of one who so freely offered to help them, by waking them, by counseling them, and by offering to help them off with their irons. And while he was troubled about this, he saw two men come tumbling over the wall, on the left side of the narrow way; and they quickly caught up with him. The name of the one was Formalist, and the name of the other Hypocrisy. So, as I said, they came up to him, and he began to talk with them like this.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Gentlemen, where have you#super[+] come from, and where are you going?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Formalist and Hypocrisy]: We were born in the land of Vain-glory, and we are going to Mount Zion, for praise.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why did you#super[+] not come in at the gate that stands at the beginning of the way? Do you#super[+] not know that it is written that “he who does not enter by the door, but climbs up some other way, is a thief and a robber”? John 10:1.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Formalist and Hypocrisy]: They said that all their countrymen thought it too far around to go to the gate to get in; and so their usual way was to take a short cut, and climb over the wall, as they had done.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But will it not be counted a trespass against the Lord of the city we are bound for, to violate his revealed will like this?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Formalist and Hypocrisy]: They told him that he need not trouble his head about that: they had custom on their side for what they did, and could produce, if need be, evidence that it had been done for more than a thousand years.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But, said Christian, will you stand up to a trial at law?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Formalist and Hypocrisy]: They told him that custom, having stood for so long, more than a thousand years, would no doubt now be accepted as lawful by an impartial judge. And besides, they said, if we get into the way, what does it matter how we get in? If we are in, we are in. You are only on the way, you who, as we can see, came in at the gate; and we too are on the way, we who came tumbling over the wall. So how is your condition any better than ours?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I walk by the rule of my Master: you#super[+] walk by the crude workings of your own fancies. You#super[+] are already counted thieves by the Lord of the way: so I fear you will not be found honest men at the end of the way. You#super[+] come in by yourselves without his direction, and you will go out by yourselves without his mercy.]

They made little answer to this; they only told him to look out for himself. Then I saw that they went on, each man on his own way, without much talk with one another, except that these two men told Christian that, as for laws and ordinances, they had no doubt they would keep them as conscientiously as he did. So, they said, we do not see how you differ from us, except by the coat on your back, which, we suppose, was given you by some of your neighbors to hide the shame of your nakedness.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: By laws and ordinances you#super[+] will not be saved, since you did not come in by the door. Gal 2:16. And as for this coat on my back, it was given me by the Lord of the place where I am going; and, as you say, it was given to cover my nakedness. And I take it as a token of kindness to me; for I had nothing but rags before. Besides, I comfort myself with it as I go. Surely, I think, when I come to the gate of the city, its Lord will know me for one of his, since I have his coat on my back; a coat that he gave me freely on the day that he stripped me of my rags. Moreover, I have a mark on my forehead, which perhaps you have not noticed, which one of my Lord’s closest companions set there on the day my burden fell off my shoulders. I will tell you, too, that I was then given a sealed roll, to comfort me as I read it on the way; I was also told to hand it in at the celestial gate, as a sign that I will surely go in after it. All these things, I suspect, you lack; and you lack them because you did not come in at the gate.]

To these things they gave him no answer; they only looked at each other and laughed. Then I saw that they all went on, except that Christian kept ahead, and he talked no more except with himself, sometimes with sighs and sometimes with comfort; he would also often read in the roll that one of the Shining Ones had given him, and it refreshed him.

Then I saw that they all went on till they came to the foot of the hill Difficulty, at the bottom of which there was a spring. In the same place there were also two other ways besides the one that came straight from the gate: one turned to the left, and the other to the right, at the bottom of the hill; but the narrow way ran straight up the hill, and the climb up the side of the hill is called Difficulty. Christian now went to the spring, Isa 49:10, and drank from it to refresh himself, and then began to go up the hill, saying,

#quote[“The hill, though high, I covet to ascend; \
The difficulty will not me offend; \
For I perceive the way to life lies here: \
Come, pluck up heart, let’s neither faint nor fear. \
Better, though #emph[difficult], the right way to go, \
Than wrong, though #emph[easy], where the end is woe.”]

The other two also came to the foot of the hill. But when they saw that the hill was steep and high, and that there were two other ways to go; and supposing too that these two ways might meet up again with the one Christian went up, on the other side of the hill; they made up their minds to go by those ways. Now one of those ways was called Danger, and the other Destruction. So one of them took the way called Danger, which led him into a great wood; and the other went straight up the way to Destruction, which led him into a wide field full of dark mountains, where he stumbled and fell, and rose no more.

Then I looked after Christian, to see him go up the hill, and I saw that he dropped from running to walking, and from walking to clambering on his hands and knees, because the place was so steep. Now about halfway to the top of the hill was a pleasant Arbor, made by the Lord of the hill for the refreshment of weary travelers. So Christian made his way there, and sat down to rest. Then he pulled his roll from inside his coat and read it, to his comfort; he also now began to look over once more the coat or garment that had been given to him as he stood by the cross. Pleasing himself like this for a while, he at last fell into a doze, and from that into a deep sleep, which kept him in that place until it was almost night; and in his sleep his roll fell out of his hand. Now, as he was sleeping, someone came to him and woke him, saying, “Go to the ant, O sluggard; observe its ways and become wise.” Prov 6:6. And at that Christian started up, and hurried on his way, and went quickly till he came to the top of the hill.

Now when he had reached the top of the hill, two men came running at full speed; the name of the one was Timorous, and of the other Mistrust. Christian said to them, Sirs, what is the matter? You#super[+] are running the wrong way. Timorous answered that they had been going to the city of Zion, and had got up that difficult place; but, he said, the farther we go, the more danger we meet; so we turned around, and we are going back again.

Yes, said Mistrust, for just ahead of us a couple of lions are lying in the way, whether asleep or awake we do not know; and we could only think that if we came within their reach they would tear us to pieces at once.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian said, You#super[+] make me afraid; but where can I flee to be safe? If I go back to my own country, that is prepared for fire and brimstone, and I will certainly perish there; if I can get to the celestial city, I am sure to be safe there: I must risk it. To go back is nothing but death; to go forward is fear of death, and everlasting life beyond it: I will still go forward. So Mistrust and Timorous ran down the hill, and Christian went on his way. But thinking again of what he had heard from the men, he felt inside his coat for his roll, so that he might read it and be comforted; but he felt, and it was not there. Then Christian was in great distress, and did not know what to do; for he lacked the thing that used to relieve him, and that should have been his pass into the celestial city. Here, then, he began to be very perplexed, and did not know what to do. At last he remembered that he had slept in the arbor on the side of the hill; and falling down on his knees, he asked God to forgive him for that foolish act, and then went back to look for his roll. But all the way back, who can fully describe the sorrow of Christian’s heart? Sometimes he sighed, sometimes he wept, and often he scolded himself for being so foolish as to fall asleep in that place, which was built only for a little refreshment from his weariness. So he went back, looking carefully on this side and on that, all the way as he went, in the hope that he might find his roll, which had been his comfort so many times on his journey. He went on like this till he came in sight of the arbor again, where he had sat and slept; but that sight renewed his sorrow all the more, by bringing the wrong of his sleeping back to his mind again, as fresh as ever. Rev 2:4; 1 Thess 5:6–8. So now he went on, lamenting his sinful sleep, and saying, O wretched man that I am, that I should sleep in the daytime! that I should sleep in the midst of difficulty! that I should so indulge the flesh as to use for my flesh’s ease the rest that the Lord of the hill built only to revive the spirits of pilgrims! How many steps have I taken in vain! This is what happened to Israel; for their sin they were sent back again by the way of the Red Sea; and I am made to tread these steps with sorrow, which I might have trodden with delight, if it had not been for this sinful sleep. How far along my way I might have been by now! I am made to tread these steps three times over, when I need only have trodden them once: yes, and now I am likely to be overtaken by night, for the day is almost spent. Oh, if only I had not slept!]

Now by this time he had come to the arbor again, where he sat down for a while and wept; but at last (as Providence would have it), looking sorrowfully down under the bench, he spotted his roll there; and trembling and in haste he snatched it up and put it inside his coat. But who can tell how joyful this man was when he had his roll again? For this roll was the assurance of his life, and of his acceptance at the haven he longed for. So he put it away safely inside his coat, thanked God for directing his eye to the place where it lay, and with joy and tears set out on his journey again. And oh, how nimbly he went up the rest of the hill! Yet before he got to the top, the sun went down on Christian; and this brought the folly of his sleeping back to his mind again; and so he began once more to lament to himself: Oh, you sinful sleep! Because of you I am likely to be overtaken by night on my journey! I must walk without the sun, darkness must cover the path of my feet, and I must hear the noise of the doleful creatures, because of my sinful sleep! Now he also remembered the story Mistrust and Timorous had told him, of how they were frightened by the sight of the lions. Then Christian said to himself again, These beasts roam in the night for their prey; and if they should meet me in the dark, how could I get away from them? How could I escape being torn to pieces by them? So he went on his way. But while he was lamenting his unhappy blunder, he looked up, and there before him was a very stately palace, whose name was Beautiful, and it stood beside the highway.

So I saw in my dream that he hurried on, so that if possible he might get lodging there. Now before he had gone far, he entered a very narrow passage, about a furlong from the Porter’s lodge; and looking very carefully ahead of him as he went, he spotted two lions in the way. Now, he thought, I see the dangers that drove Mistrust and Timorous back. (The lions were chained, but he did not see the chains.) Then he was afraid, and he too thought of going back after them; for he thought nothing but death was before him. But the Porter at the lodge, whose name is Watchful, seeing that Christian had stopped as if he would go back, called out to him, saying, Is your strength so small? Mark 4:40. Do not fear the lions, for they are chained, and they are placed there to test faith where there is any, and to expose those who have none. Keep to the middle of the path, and no harm shall come to you.

Then I saw that he went on, trembling for fear of the lions, but carefully following the Porter’s directions; he heard them roar, but they did him no harm. Then he clapped his hands, and went on till he came and stood before the gate where the Porter was. Then Christian said to the Porter, Sir, what house is this? And may I stay here tonight? The Porter answered, This house was built by the Lord of the hill, and he built it for the relief and safety of pilgrims. The Porter also asked where he was from, and where he was going.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I have come from the city of Destruction, and I am going to Mount Zion; but because the sun has now set, I would like, if I may, to stay here tonight.]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Porter]: What is your name?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: My name is now Christian, but my name at first was Graceless: I am of the race of Japheth, whom God will persuade to dwell in the tents of Shem. Gen 9:27.]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Porter]: But how is it that you come so late? The sun has set.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I would have been here sooner, except that, wretched man that I am, I slept in the arbor that stands on the hillside! Indeed, even so, I would have been here much sooner, except that in my sleep I lost my evidence, and came without it to the brow of the hill; and then, feeling for it and not finding it, I was forced with sorrow of heart to go back to the place where I slept my sleep, and there I found it; and now here I am.]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Porter]: Well, I will call out one of the young women of this place, who will, if she likes your talk, bring you in to the rest of the family, according to the rules of the house. So Watchful the Porter rang a bell, and at the sound of it a grave and beautiful young woman named Discretion came out of the door of the house, and asked why she had been called.]

The Porter answered, This man is on a journey from the city of Destruction to Mount Zion; but being weary and overtaken by night, he asked me if he might stay here tonight. So I told him I would call for you, and after talking with him you may do as you think best, according to the law of the house.

Then she asked him where he was from, and where he was going; and he told her. She also asked him how he got into the way; and he told her. Then she asked him what he had seen and met with on the way; and he told her. And at last she asked his name. So he said, It is Christian; and I want to stay here tonight all the more because, from what I can see, this place was built by the Lord of the hill for the relief and safety of pilgrims. So she smiled, but tears stood in her eyes; and after a little pause she said, I will call out two or three more of the family. So she ran to the door, and called out Prudence, Piety, and Charity, who, after talking with him a little more, brought him into the family; and many of them, meeting him at the threshold of the house, said, Come in, you blessed of the Lord; this house was built by the Lord of the hill for the very purpose of lodging such pilgrims as you. Then he bowed his head, and followed them into the house. So when he had come in and sat down, they gave him something to drink, and agreed among themselves that, until supper was ready, some of them should have a particular conversation with Christian, to make the best use of the time; and they appointed Piety, Prudence, and Charity to talk with him. And so they began.

#speech[#smallcaps[Piety]: Come, good Christian, since we have been so loving to you as to receive you into our house tonight, let us talk with you, in the hope that we may better ourselves by it, about all the things that have happened to you on your pilgrimage.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Very willingly; and I am glad that you are so inclined.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Piety]: What first moved you to take up a pilgrim’s life?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I was driven out of my native country by a dreadful sound that was in my ears: namely, that unavoidable destruction awaited me if I stayed in the place where I was.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Piety]: But how did it happen that you came out of your country this way?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: It was as God would have it; for when I was under the fear of destruction, I did not know where to go; but by chance a man came, right up to me, as I was trembling and weeping, whose name is Evangelist; and he directed me to the Wicket-gate, which I would never have found otherwise, and so set me on the way that has led me straight to this house.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Piety]: But did you not come by the house of the Interpreter?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, and I saw such things there that the memory of them will stay with me as long as I live; especially three things: namely, how Christ, in spite of Satan, keeps up his work of grace in the heart; how the man had sinned himself completely out of hope of God’s mercy; and also the dream of the man who thought in his sleep that the day of judgment had come.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Piety]: Why, did you hear him tell his dream?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, and a dreadful one it was, I thought; it made my heart ache as he was telling it, but still I am glad I heard it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Piety]: Was this all you saw at the house of the Interpreter?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: No; he took me and brought me to where he showed me a stately palace, and how the people in it were dressed in gold; and how a daring man came and cut his way through the armed men who stood in the door to keep him out; and how he was told to come in and win eternal glory. Those things, I thought, enraptured my heart. I would have stayed at that good man’s house a whole year, except that I knew I had farther to go.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Piety]: And what else did you see on the way?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Saw? Why, I went only a little farther, and I saw One, as I thought in my mind, hanging bleeding on a tree; and the very sight of him made my burden fall off my back; for I was groaning under a very heavy burden, but then it fell right off me. It was a strange thing to me, for I had never seen such a thing before. Yes, and while I stood looking up (for then I could not stop looking), three Shining Ones came to me. One of them declared that my sins were forgiven me; another stripped me of my rags, and gave me this embroidered coat which you see; and the third set the mark which you see on my forehead, and gave me this sealed roll (and with that he pulled it out from inside his coat).]

#speech[#smallcaps[Piety]: But you saw more than this, did you not?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: The things that I have told you were the best; yet I saw some other things. For instance, I saw three men, Simple, Sloth, and Presumption, lying asleep a little off the way as I came, with irons on their heels; but do you think I could wake them? I also saw Formalist and Hypocrisy come tumbling over the wall, to go, as they claimed, to Zion; but they were soon lost, just as I myself told them, but they would not believe me. But above all, I found it hard work to get up this hill, and just as hard to get past the lions’ mouths; and truly, if it had not been for the good man, the porter who stands at the gate, I might, for all I know, have gone back again after all; but I thank God I am here, and I thank you for receiving me.]

Then Prudence thought it good to ask him a few questions, and asked him to answer them.

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Do you not sometimes think of the country you came from?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, but with great shame and loathing. Truly, if I had been mindful of that country I came out of, I might have had the opportunity to return; but now I desire a better country, that is, a heavenly one. Heb 11:15, 16.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Do you not still carry with you some of the things you were taken up with then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, but very much against my will; especially my inward and carnal thoughts, which all my countrymen, as well as I, delighted in. But now all those things are my grief; and if I could only choose for myself, I would choose never to think of those things again. But when I want to do what is best, what is worst is with me. Rom 7:15, 21.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Do you not sometimes find those things seem to be defeated, which at other times are your distress?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, but that is only seldom; but the hours when such things happen to me are golden hours to me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Can you remember what it is that makes you find your troubles at times as if they were defeated?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes: when I think of what I saw at the cross, that will do it; and when I look at my embroidered coat, that will do it; and when I look into the roll that I carry inside my coat, that will do it; and when my thoughts grow warm about where I am going, that will do it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: And what is it that makes you so eager to go to Mount Zion?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, there I hope to see Him alive who hung dead on the cross; and there I hope to be rid of all those things that to this day are in me and trouble me. There, they say, there is no death, Isa 25:8; Rev 21:4; and there I will live with the kind of company I like best. For, to tell you the truth, I love Him because He eased me of my burden; and I am weary of my inward sickness. I long to be where I will die no more, and with the company that will continually cry, #emph[Holy, holy, holy.]]

Then Charity said to Christian, Do you have a family? Are you a married man?

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I have a wife and four small children.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Charity]: And why did you not bring them along with you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian wept, and said, Oh, how gladly I would have done it! But every one of them was utterly against my going on pilgrimage.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Charity]: But you should have talked to them, and tried to show them the danger of staying behind.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: So I did; and I also told them what God had shown me of the destruction of our city; but they thought I was joking, and they did not believe me. Gen 19:14.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Charity]: And did you pray to God that he would bless your advice to them?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, and with deep feeling; for you can imagine that my wife and poor children were very dear to me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Charity]: But did you tell them of your own sorrow, and your fear of destruction? For I suppose the destruction was plain enough to you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, over, and over, and over. They could also see my fears in my face, in my tears, and in my trembling under the dread of the judgment that hung over our heads; but all of it was not enough to persuade them to come with me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Charity]: But what reason could they give for themselves for not coming?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, my wife was afraid of losing this world, and my children were given over to the foolish pleasures of youth; so, what with one thing and what with another, they left me to wander alone like this.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Charity]: But did you not, by your vain life, dampen all the persuasion you used in words to bring them away with you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Indeed, I cannot commend my life, for I know of many failings in it. I know too that a man, by his conduct, may soon overthrow what he labors, by argument or persuasion, to press on others for their good. Yet this I can say: I was very careful not to give them cause, by any unbecoming action, to turn them against going on pilgrimage. Yes, for this very thing they would tell me I was too strict, and that I denied myself things (for their sakes) in which they saw no harm. Indeed, I think I may say that if what they saw in me hindered them, it was my great tenderness about sinning against God, or about doing any wrong to my neighbor.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Charity]: Indeed, Cain hated his brother, because his own works were evil, and his brother’s righteous, 1 John 3:12; and if your wife and children have been offended with you for this, they show by it that they are stubbornly hostile to good; you have delivered your soul from their blood. Ezek 3:19.]

Now I saw in my dream that they sat talking together like this until supper was ready. So when everything was ready, they sat down to eat. Now the table was spread with rich food, and with wine that was well refined; and all their talk at the table was about the Lord of the hill: about what he had done, and why he did what he did, and why he had built that house. And from what they said, I understood that he had been a great warrior, and had fought with and killed him who had the power of death, Heb 2:14, 15; but not without great danger to himself, which made me love him all the more.

For, as they said, and as I believe, said Christian, he did it with the loss of much blood. But what put the glory of grace into all he did was that he did it out of pure love for his country. And besides, some of the household said they had been with him and spoken with him since he died on the cross; and they have declared that they heard it from his own lips, that he is such a lover of poor pilgrims that his like is not to be found from the east to the west. They also gave an example of what they affirmed; and it was this: he had stripped himself of his glory so that he might do this for the poor; and they had heard him say and affirm that he would not live on the mountain of Zion alone. They said, too, that he had made many pilgrims princes, though by nature they were born beggars, and had come from the dunghill. 1 Sam 2:8; Ps 113:7.

So they talked together till late at night; and after they had committed themselves to their Lord for protection, they went to bed. They put the pilgrim in a large upstairs room, whose window opened towards the sunrise. The name of the room was Peace; there he slept till daybreak, and then he woke and sang,

#quote[“Where am I now? Is this the love and care \
Of Jesus, for the men that pilgrims are, \
Thus to provide that I should be forgiven, \
And dwell already the next door to heaven!”]

So in the morning they all got up; and after some more talk, they told him that he must not leave till they had shown him the rare things of that place. And first they took him into the study, where they showed him records of the greatest age; in which, as I remember my dream, they showed him the family line of the Lord of the hill: that he was the Son of the Ancient of Days, and came by eternal generation. Here, too, the deeds he had done were recorded more fully, and the names of many hundreds whom he had taken into his service; and how he had placed them in such dwellings as could be destroyed neither by length of days nor by the decay of nature.

Then they read to him some of the noble deeds that some of his servants had done: how they had subdued kingdoms, worked righteousness, obtained promises, stopped the mouths of lions, quenched the violence of fire, escaped the edge of the sword, out of weakness were made strong, became valiant in battle, and turned to flight the armies of the aliens. Heb 11:33, 34.

Then they read another part of the records of the house, which showed how willing their Lord was to receive anyone into his favor, even anyone, though in the past they had greatly insulted his person and his dealings. Here also were various other histories of many other famous things, all of which Christian looked at: things both ancient and modern, together with prophecies and predictions of things that are certain to be fulfilled, both to the dread and dismay of enemies, and to the comfort and encouragement of pilgrims.

The next day they took him into the armory, where they showed him all kinds of equipment which their Lord had provided for pilgrims: sword, shield, helmet, breastplate, all-prayer, and shoes that would not wear out. And there was enough of this here to equip as many men for the service of their Lord as there are stars in the sky in all their multitude.

They also showed him some of the instruments with which some of his servants had done wonderful things. They showed him Moses’ rod; the hammer and nail with which Jael killed Sisera; and the pitchers, trumpets, and lamps too, with which Gideon put to flight the armies of Midian. Then they showed him the ox-goad with which Shamgar killed six hundred men. They also showed him the jawbone with which Samson did such mighty feats. They showed him, too, the sling and stone with which David killed Goliath of Gath; and also the sword with which their Lord will kill the man of sin, on the day that he rises up to the prey. Besides these they showed him many excellent things, which delighted Christian greatly. When this was done, they went to bed again.

Then I saw in my dream that the next morning he got up to go on, but they wanted him to stay till the following day as well; and then, they said, if the day is clear, we will show you the Delectable Mountains; which, they said, would add still more to his comfort, because they were nearer the haven he longed for than the place where he was now. So he agreed and stayed. When morning came, they took him up to the top of the house, and told him to look south. So he did, and there, at a great distance, he saw a most pleasant mountainous country, beautiful with woods, vineyards, fruits of all kinds, and flowers too, with springs and fountains, very delectable to look at. Isa 33:16, 17. Then he asked the name of the country. They said it was Immanuel’s land; and it is as open, they said, as this hill is, to all the pilgrims. And when you get there, you can see from there as far as the gate of the celestial city, as the shepherds who live there will show you.

Now he thought it was time to set out, and they were willing that he should. But first, they said, let us go into the armory again. So they did; and when he got there, they armed him from head to foot with proven armor, in case he should meet with attacks on the way. So, equipped like this, he walked out to the gate with his friends; and there he asked the Porter if he had seen any pilgrim go by. Then the Porter answered, Yes.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Tell me, did you know him? he said.]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Porter]: I asked his name, and he told me it was Faithful.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Oh, said Christian, I know him; he is from my town, my close neighbor; he comes from the place where I was born. How far ahead do you think he may be?]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Porter]: By now he has got below the hill.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, said Christian, good Porter, the Lord be with you, and add much increase to all your plain blessings, for the kindness that you have shown me.]
